bool b1, bool b2, Args &&...args) {    \\\n        return name##_impl<max_n>(n, b1, b2, std::forward<Args>(args)...);                         \\\n    }\n```\n\n**Usage** (from zerocheck_round0.cu line 594):\n```cuda\nDEFINE_DISPATCH_N_B1_B2(dispatch_zerocheck, launch_zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints, 4)\n```\n\nThis creates runtime dispatching for `num_cosets` values 1-4 combined with two boolean flags (is_global, needs_shmem)."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"48b5d5bc-06ba-4812-85f4-27a650fcd6d2","uuid":"fd452125-71df-4af3-9c5d-f2e17b2655b8","timestamp":"2026-04-14T04:40:12.179Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the CUDA-side type definitions used in this project. Find and report:\n\n1. The FpExt type definition on the CUDA side - search in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/` and `crates/cuda-common/cuda/include/` for FpExt, Fp4, BabyBear, etc. I need to know the exact type name used in .cu files for the extension field.\n\n2. The Frac type on CUDA side - how is `Frac<EF>` represented in CUDA? Search for `Frac`, `FracExt`, etc. in .cu and .cuh files.\n\n3. Look at any existing .cu file in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/` (e.g., `zerocheck_round0.cu` or `logup_round0.cu`) to see:\n   - What headers they include\n   - What namespace conventions they use\n   - How they access FpExt fields (is it `.c0`, `.c1`, etc.?)\n   - How arithmetic operations are performed on FpExt\n\n4. Look at `crates/cuda-common/cuda/include/` for `launcher.cuh` to understand the kernel launch macro/pattern.\n\n5. Check how `current_stream_sync()` is defined in the Rust code (search in cuda-common).\n\n6. Look at how existing `extern \"C\"` functions are defined in .cu files - the pattern for return type (int), naming convention with underscore prefix, etc.\n\nReport exact file paths, line numbers, and snippets. Be thorough.","agentId":"a8a6c01f9869ad010","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information I need. Let me compile the comprehensive report:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've thoroughly analyzed the CUDA-side type definitions and patterns in this stark-backend project. Here are my detailed findings:\n\n### 1. FpExt Type Definition\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/include/fpext.h`\n\n**Type Definition (lines 38-122)**:\n```cuda\nstruct FpExt {\n    /// The elements of FpExt, elems[0] + elems[1]*X + elems[2]*X^2 + elems[3]*x^4\n    union {\n        Fp elems[4];\n        bb31_4_t rep;\n    };\n    // ... constructors and operators\n};\n```\n\n**Key Facts**:\n- `FpExt` represents elements of a finite field F_p^4\n- Based on the irreducible polynomial x^4 - 11\n- Represents elements as: `elems[0] + elems[1]*X + elems[2]*X^2 + elems[3]*X^3`\n- You access fields using: `.elems[0]`, `.elems[1]`, `.elems[2]`, `.elems[3]` (NOT `.c0`, `.c1`, etc.)\n- Can also use `.rep` for the underlying `bb31_4_t` representation\n- Size: 16 bytes (4 * 4-byte Fp values)\n\n### 2. Frac Type (Extension Field Fraction)\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/frac_ext.cuh`\n\n**Type Definition (lines 6-9)**:\n```cuda\nstruct FracExt {\n    FpExt p;\n    FpExt q;\n};\n```\n\n**Key Facts**:\n- Represents a fraction in the extension field as numerator/denominator\n- `FracExt.p` is the numerator (FpExt)\n- `FracExt.q` is the denominator (FpExt)\n- Provides helper functions: `frac_add()`, `frac_unadd()`, `frac_add_inplace()`, `frac_unadd_inplace()`\n- Example usage in logup_round0.cu (line 292): `sums[c].p += eq * numer_results[c];` and `sums[c].q += eq * (denom_results[c] + denom_sum_init);`\n\n### 3. Existing .cu File Examples\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu`\n\n**Headers (lines 1-16)**:\n```cuda\n#include \"codec.cuh\"\n#include \"dag_entry.cuh\"\n#include \"eval_config.cuh\"\n#include \"fp.h\"\n#include \"fpext.h\"\n#include \"frac_ext.cuh\"\n#include \"launcher.cuh\"\n#include \"sumcheck.cuh\"\n#include \"utils.cuh\"\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <cmath>\n#include <cstddef>\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <utility>\n#include <vector_types.h>\n\nusing namespace symbolic_dag;\n```\n\n**Namespace Convention (line 19)**:\n- Typically wrapped in `namespace logup_round0 { ... }` or `namespace zerocheck_round0 { ... }`\n\n**FpExt Field Access Pattern (lines 290-293)**:\n```cuda\nfor (uint32_t c = 0; c < NUM_COSETS; c++) {\n    sums[c].p += eq * numer_results[c];\n    sums[c].q += eq * (denom_results[c] + denom_sum_init);\n}\n```\n\n**FracExt Array Operations (lines 307-308)**:\n```cuda\ntile_sum.p += shared_sum[lane_offset].p;\ntile_sum.q += shared_sum[lane_offset].q;\n```\n\n### 4. Launcher Pattern - launcher.cuh\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/include/launcher.cuh`\n\n**Kernel Launch Helper (lines 14-20)**:\n```cuda\ninline std::pair<dim3, dim3> kernel_launch_params(\n    size_t count,\n    size_t threads_per_block = MAX_THREADS\n) {\n    size_t block = std::min(count, threads_per_block);\n    size_t grid = div_ceil(count, block);\n    return std::make_pair(dim3(grid, 1, 1), dim3(block, 1, 1));\n}\n```\n\n**Launch Usage Pattern** (from zerocheck_round0.cu lines 555-575):\n```cuda\nzerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_kernel<NUM_COSETS, GLOBAL, NEEDS_SHMEM>\n    <<<grid, block, shmem_bytes>>>(\n        tmp_sums_buffer,\n        selectors_cube,\n        // ... more parameters\n    );\nint err = CHECK_KERNEL();\nif (err != 0) {\n    return err;\n}\n```\n\n**Coset Config Pattern** (from eval_config.cuh):\n- `coset_round0_config::eval_constraints_launch_params()` for lockstep mode\n- `coset_parallel_round0_config::eval_constraints_launch_params()` for coset-parallel mode\n\n### 5. current_stream_sync() in Rust\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/stream.rs`\n\n**Definition (lines 81-83)**:\n```rust\npub fn current_stream_sync() -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n    check(unsafe { cudaStreamSynchronize(cudaStreamPerThread) })\n}\n```\n\n**Usage Context**:\n- Synchronizes the current CUDA thread's default stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`)\n- Returns `Result<(), CudaError>`\n- Used after kernel launches to ensure completion\n\n### 6. extern \"C\" Function Patterns\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/device_info.cu` (simple example)\n\n**Pattern (lines 6-16)**:\n```cuda\nextern \"C\" int _cuda_get_sm_count(uint32_t device_id, uint32_t *out_sm_count) {\n    int sm_count = 0;\n    cudaError_t err = cudaDeviceGetAttribute(\n        &sm_count, cudaDevAttrMultiProcessorCount, static_cast<int>(device_id)\n    );\n    if (err != cudaSuccess) {\n        return static_cast<int>(err);\n    }\n    *out_sm_count = static_cast<uint32_t>(sm_count);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n**Key Characteristics**:\n- Return type: `int` (0 for success, non-zero error code)\n- Function name format: `_function_name_snake_case` (underscore prefix)\n- Parameters passed by value or pointer (for output)\n- Direct return of error codes using `CHECK_KERNEL()` or manual error handling\n\n**Kernel Launch Example** (from batch_ntt_small.cu lines 53-71):\n```cuda\nextern \"C\" int _generate_device_ntt_twiddles(Fp *d_twiddles) {\n    // Generate twiddles on GPU\n    constexpr uint32_t BLOCK_SIZE = 256;\n    uint32_t num_blocks = div_ceil(DEVICE_NTT_TWIDDLES_SIZE, BLOCK_SIZE);\n    generate_device_ntt_twiddles_kernel<<<num_blocks, BLOCK_SIZE>>>(d_twiddles);\n\n    // Copy to constant memory using per-thread stream\n    cudaMemcpyToSymbolAsync(\n        DEVICE_NTT_TWIDDLES,\n        d_twiddles,\n        DEVICE_NTT_TWIDDLES_SIZE * sizeof(Fp),\n        0,\n        cudaMemcpyDeviceToDevice,\n        cudaStreamPerThread\n    );\n    cudaStreamSynchronize(cudaStreamPerThread);\n\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n### 7. Dispatcher Macro Pattern\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/utils.cuh`\n\n**Macro Definition (lines 58-77)**:\n```cuda\n#define DEFINE_DISPATCH_N_B1_B2(name, func, max_n)                                                 \\\n    template <uint32_t N, typename... Args>                                                        \\\n    inline int name##_impl(uint32_t n, bool b1, bool b2, Args &&...args) {                         \\\n        if (n == N) {                                                                              \\\n            if (b1) {                                                                              \\\n                return b2 ? func<N, true, true>(std::forward<Args>(args)...)                       \\\n                          : func<N, true, false>(std::forward<Args>(args)...);                     \\\n            } else {                                                                               \\\n                return b2 ? func<N, false, true>(std::forward<Args>(args)...)                      \\\n                          : func<N, false, false>(std::forward<Args>(args)...);                    \\\n            }                                                                                      \\\n        } else if constexpr (N == 1) {                                                             \\\n            return -1;                                                                             \\\n        } else {                                                                                   \\\n            return name##_impl<N - 1>(n, b1, b2, std::forward<Args>(args)...);                     \\\n        }                                                                                          \\\n    }                                                                                              \\\n    template <typename... Args> inline int name(uint32_t n, bool b1, bool b2, Args &&...args) {    \\\n        return name##_impl<max_n>(n, b1, b2, std::forward<Args>(args)...);                         \\\n    }\n```\n\n**Usage** 