batch the D2H transfers to eliminate per-AIR synchronization overhead.\n\nActually, the GPU-side approach is cleaner—I'll implement the transformation matrix computation directly in CUDA. For the zerocheck transform, I need to handle the input as a flat vector in coset-major order and produce polynomial coefficients. I can compute this column by column by transposing the input into a matrix and applying iDFT to each column.\n\nRather than computing each step separately, I'll compose the entire pipeline as a single transformation matrix: transpose (permutation), iDFT (block-diagonal), unshift (diagonal scaling), Lagrange interpolation, and coefficient adjustment. This lets me compute each element of the final transform matrix directly without materializing intermediate results.\n\nActually, the simplest approach is to just apply the existing `from_geometric_cosets_evals_idft` function to unit vectors to extract the transform matrix. But I'm realizing the normalization step in the logup case is AIR-specific, so it can't be baked into the matrix itself—it needs to be applied separately. Since normalization is just scalar multiplication and matrix multiplication is linear, I can apply the norm factor either before or after the transform without changing the result, so the GPU kernel can handle this flexibly.\n\nNow I'm ready to finalize the design and start implementing. I'll precompute the transform matrices on the CPU for each unique constraint degree, calculating the appropriate dimensions and roots of unity needed for the zerocheck transform.\n\nBuilding the transform matrix...\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a type mismatch—the `from_geometric_cosets_evals_idft` function expects base field elements for the shift and init parameters, but I'm passing extension field elements. I need to check the function signature to understand what types are actually required here. I see the iDFT is operating on extension field elements, and the unshifting step multiplies those by base